Cincinnati, poses with the remains of a Greek warrior unearthed in southwest 
Greece.\")\n\n# **Lord of the rings**\n\n## *A University of Cincinnati team's 
rare discovery of four gold rings in the tomb of a wealthy Bronze Age warrior 
undisturbed for 3,500 years prompts a new consideration of Greek 
history.*\n\n[by Rachel Richardson](mailto: rachel.richardson@uc.edu)  \n 
513-556-5219\n\n**Oct. 3, 2016**\n\n![UC's Sharon Stocker, left, and Jack Davis,
right, have worked in the Pylos region of Greece for 25 years. They led a team 
of 45 archaeologists and experts in various specialties as well as students 
during this summer's excavations. Stocker stands in the shaft tomb the team 
uncovered.](https://magazine.uc.edu/editors_picks/recent_features/lordoftherings
/jcr%3acontent/MainContent/responsiveparsys/1439395153466/textimage/image.img.jp
g/1539867750004.jpg \"UC's Shari Stocker, left, and Jack Davis, right, have 
worked in the Pylos region of Greece for 25 years. Stocker stands in the tomb of
a Greek warrior uncovered by the UC-based team.\")\n\nUC's Shari Stocker, left, 
and Jack Davis, right, have worked in the Pylos region of Greece for 25 years. 
Stocker stands in the tomb of a Greek warrior uncovered by the UC-based 
team.\n\nWhen University of Cincinnati researchers [uncovered the tomb of a 
Bronze Age 
warrior](http://magazine.uc.edu/editors_picks/recent_features/warrior_tomb.html)
— left untouched for more than 3,500 years and packed with a spectacular array 
of precious jewelry, weapons and riches — the discovery was hailed by experts as
“the find of a lifetime.”\n\nNow, only a year after archaeologists completed the
excavation, new understandings of the artifacts — particularly the discovery of 
four golden rings — and the insights they provide to the origins of Greek 
civilization may prove to be the team's next big discovery.\n\nShari Stocker, a 
senior research associate in UC's Department of Classics, and Jack Davis, the 
university's Carl W. Blegen chair in Greek archaeology, will reveal the UC-based
team's findings from the so-called [\"Griffin 
Warrior\"](http://www.griffinwarrior.org/) grave Thursday, Oct. 6, at The 
American School  of Classical Studies at Athens, Greece.\n\nThe husband-and-wife
team's highly anticipated lecture is generating worldwide attention, including 
[a feature in the New York 
Times](http://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/04/science/greece-archaeology-pylos-griffi
n-warrior.html?_r=0).\n\n![The UC-based team excavates the tomb of a Greek 
warrior discovered in southwest 
Greece.](https://magazine.uc.edu/editors_picks/recent_features/lordoftherings/jc
