## 1. Evans, “‘The Ring of Nestor’: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and a 
Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia” 
(Journal of Hellenic Studies)

- **Title**: “‘The Ring of Nestor’: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and A 
Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia”
- **URL**: 
https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-hellenic-studies/article/ring
-of-nestor-a-glimpse-into-the-minoan-afterworld-and-a-sepulchral-treasure-of-gol
d-signetrings-and-beadseals-from-thisbe-boeotia/5E465BD1031E9251C962E138498E6CFA
- **Author / Publisher**: Sir Arthur Evans; Journal of Hellenic Studies 
(Cambridge University Press)
- **Date**: Original JHS article 1925 (online publication by Cambridge 
University Press listed as 23 December 2013; the journal issue is vol. 45, 1925)
- **Source type**: Peer‑reviewed archaeology / classics journal article (primary
scholarly publication)
- **Query that triggered it**: `"Ring of Nestor" tholos tomb Kakovatos Pylos 
gold signet ring`
- **Relevance**:
  - Core primary study that formally names and describes the “Ring of Nestor” as
an archaeological object.
  - Provides discovery circumstances (time, place, finder), physical description
(gold signet ring, iconography, etc.), archaeological context (Mycenaean 
rock‑cut chamber tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia), dating and cultural attribution, 
and Evans’s interpretation of its religious/ritual significance.
- **Key points / faithful summary**:
  - Evans introduces a “truly royal treasure of gold rings and of bead-seals in 
the same metal” found in a Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near the site of 
Thisbê in Boeotia, by the village of Dombrena.
  - He notes that the treasure was discovered in 1915 during World War I, when 
“war conditions diverted the course of discovery from official channels.” He 
says the find was made by a peasant in a rock‑cut chamber tomb, and that for 
reasons of confidence not all details can be disclosed (indicating a 
non‑official, semi‑clandestine discovery).
  - The article explains that Evans was able to see and secure the hoard, which 
includes several gold signet rings and bead‑seals, and that he presents them as 
a coherent sepulchral treasure.
  - Among the rings, Evans singles out one especially large gold signet ring, 
which he dubs the “Ring of Nestor.” The name alludes to the Homeric king Nestor 
and his association with Pylos, but here refers to a prestigious Mycenaean 
object from Boeotia; Evans uses the name as a poetic label rather than a literal
owner attribution.
  - Evans describes the ring as having a “beaded” or granulated gold hoop and a 
large oval bezel engraved in relief with a complex scene in Minoan style. He 
stresses its massive size and craftsmanship, comparing it with other known gold 
signet rings.
  - The engraved scene on the bezel shows what Evans interprets as a Minoan‑type
ritual or funerary scene in an “after‑world” setting. The composition includes 
multiple figures arranged in successive registers or “three successive scenes” 
along the bezel, which Evans reads in sequence.
  - He identifies elements such as deities or officiants, altars or cult 
structures, and symbols suggestive of the Minoan cult of the dead. He argues 
that the iconography offers a “glimpse” into Minoan beliefs about the afterlife 
and that the ring may have functioned as a kind of eschatological or funerary 
object.
  - Evans dates the treasure and the ring to the late Mycenaean period (Late 
Helladic) but emphasizes strong Minoan influence in style and iconography, 
suggesting that Boeotian elites adopted or imported Minoan ritual imagery.
  - He notes associated objects from the same group of tombs, such as bronze 
spear‑heads and a short sword of a “very late Mycenaean type,” which help to 
place the treasure chronologically.
  - The article situates the ring within the broader corpus of Aegean gold 
signet rings and compares its iconography to other known Minoan and Mycenaean 
seals, arguing for a shared symbolic vocabulary.
  - Evans presents the ring as genuine and highly significant for understanding 
Minoan‑Mycenaean religious and funerary practices, though he acknowledges the 
irregular circumstances of its discovery and acquisition.
  
