## 7. Metropolitan Museum of Art – “Historic Images of the Greek Bronze Age”

- **Title**: “Historic Images of the Greek Bronze Age”
- **URL**: 
https://www.metmuseum.org/perspectives/historic-images-of-the-greek-bronze-age
- **Author / Publisher**: Nanno Marinatos; The Metropolitan Museum of Art
- **Date**: 17 May 2011
- **Source type**: Museum essay / scholarly web article
- **Query that triggered it**: ““Ring of Nestor” archaeological object discovery
description”
- **Relevance**:
  - Discusses the Ring of Nestor (and the Ring of Minos) in the context of early
20th‑century reproductions and possible forgeries by the draughtsman‑restorer 
Émile Gilliéron fils.
  - Provides high‑level interpretive context and authenticity debates.
- **Key points / faithful summary**:
  - Marinatos examines early 20th‑century drawings, reconstructions, and casts 
of Aegean Bronze Age artifacts created by E. Gilliéron fils, a professional 
restorer and copyist who worked closely with Sir Arthur Evans.
  - She argues that some well‑known objects, including the “Ring of Minos” and 
the “Ring of Nestor,” may actually be modern creations or heavily “restored” 
pieces produced by Gilliéron.
  - The essay states that Marinatos has put forward “compelling arguments that 
the Ring of Minos and the Ring of Nestor were forgeries by E. Gilliéron, fils.”
  - This places the Ring of Nestor squarely within an authenticity controversy, 
suggesting that:
    - Its supposed Bronze Age origin has been challenged.
    - The design may have been invented or significantly embellished by a modern
restorer, albeit one deeply immersed in Bronze Age iconography.
  - Marinatos discusses how these questionable objects nevertheless shaped 
modern understandings of Minoan iconography, influencing both scholarly 
reconstructions and public perceptions of the Bronze Age Aegean.
  - The essay highlights the need to reassess the evidentiary value of such 
rings when reconstructing ancient religious and artistic traditions.

