    {
      "local_ref": "d7",
      "document_summary": "This text of Arthur Evans’s lecture “The Minoan 
World,” delivered at the Royal Academy of Arts and later made available by the 
Aegeus Society, discusses various aspects of Minoan civilization, including art,
cultural connections and glyptic material. Within a broader narrative about 
Cretan and Nilotic connections and the development of Minoan culture, Evans 
refers to signet rings and bead-seals from different sites, including the Thisbê
material from Boeotia. He mentions a “Thisbê jewel” and compares scenes such as 
lion‑spearing compositions and compound spear shafts on signet rings from the 
Fourth Shaft Grave at Mycenae and a gold bead from a Boeotian peasant obtained 
for the Ashmolean Museum. These comparative references show how Evans situates 
the Boeotian jewellery, which includes the Ring of Nestor, within the wider 
corpus of Minoan–Mycenaean glyptic and uses them to argue for cultural 
interactions between Crete and mainland Greece. The lecture thus provides 
contextual information on how the Thisbê treasure and related artefacts were 
integrated into Evans’s broader picture of the Minoan world and its artistic and
religious themes.",
