{"query": {"id": "20260628153519549507_f8d73d29", "handle": "q1", "text": "“Ring
of Nestor” scholarly article", "importance": "core"}, "items": [{"id": 
"20260628153824552161_3dc3867e", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The digitized 
volume is titled: \"\"The ring of Nestor\". A glimpse into the Minoan 
after-world and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from 
Thisbê, Boeotia.\" The contents include: I. Sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia (pp. 1–42). II. A glimpse into 
the Minoan after-world (pp. 43–75).", "source_ids": 
["20260628153822814854_789fd2dd"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": 
"20260628153824624018_6b840cea", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Keywords in the 
Heidelberg metadata explicitly link the publication to Nestor, Ring and Minoan 
culture and list Thísvi (Thisbê, Boeotia), Kakóvatos and broader Europe and 
Greece as places.", "source_ids": ["20260628153822814854_789fd2dd"], 
"citations": [8]}, {"id": "20260628153824696375_7ae47e9c", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The abstract snippet for Pini’s article states: \"Here new technical 
observations are presented which serve to support the authenticity of the Ring 
of Nestor.\"", "source_ids": ["20260628153822843474_07c7f954"], "citations": 
[9]}, {"id": "20260628153824769367_4b25c00b", "type": "Evidence", "text": "A 
second Wiley snippet repeats: \"Here new technical observations are presented 
which serve to support the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor. In 1995 the ring 
was once again ...\" (truncated).", "source_ids": 
["20260628153822843474_07c7f954"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628153824840135_63c815b0", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The ResearchGate 
snippet cites: \"The Ring of Nestor (Evans 1925(Evans , 1930 Pini 1998; 
Krzyszkowska 2005, 334- suggest that this is not a realistic scene, but rather a
mythical or ...\" (truncated).", "source_ids": 
["20260628153822877550_2fda6fc9"], "citations": [10]}, {"id": 
"20260628153824927079_da1ce3c7", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Academia.edu 
description notes: \"This paper presents an analysis of the iconography 
surrounding the 'Ring of Nestor' in the context of Aegean forgeries and 
authenticity debates.\"", "source_ids": ["20260628153737636391_c117a38b"], 
"citations": [4]}, {"id": "20260628153825012342_52b06f8a", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The HathiTrust summary describes the work as: \"ʻThe ring of Nestor;̓ a
glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia, by Sir Arthur ...\"", 
"source_ids": ["20260628153822927050_ba50f435"], "citations": [11]}, {"id": 
"20260628153825079076_60a7780c", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The National 
Library of Ireland catalog snippet reads: \"The ring of Nestor : a glimpse into 
the Minoan after-world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and 
bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia /.\"", "source_ids": 
["20260628153822955358_30fd3086"], "citations": [12]}, {"id": 
"20260628153825149445_265c30fb", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In the Aegeus 
Society PDF, Evans mentions \"the Thisbê jewel\" and an amygdaloid bead-seal 
from Knossos as parallels, and refers to a lion‑spearing scene and a compound 
spear shaft comparable to signet rings from the Fourth Shaft Grave at Mycenae 
and a gold bead from a Boeotian peasant acquired for the Ashmolean Museum.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628153822989244_84d15031"], "citations": [13]}, {"id": 
"20260628153850799588_1c3f95e3", "type": "Claim", "text": "Arthur J. Evans’s 
monograph “The ring of Nestor: a glimpse into the Minoan after‑world, and a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia” 
presents the Ring of Nestor as the most prominent item in a Late Bronze Age 
treasure of gold signet rings and bead-seals found at or near Thisbê (Thísvi) in
Boeotia, and treats it as a genuine Aegean artefact.", "source_ids": 
["20260628153822814854_789fd2dd", "20260628153822927050_ba50f435", 
"20260628153822955358_30fd3086"], "citations": [8, 11, 12]}, {"id": 
"20260628153850974586_d07d47a2", "type": "Claim", "text": "Evans provides a 
detailed physical and iconographic description of the Ring of Nestor as a gold 
signet ring engraved with a complex scene including human figures, a chariot, 
landscape elements and symbolic motifs, and he interprets this scene as an 
otherworldly or funerary vision of the Minoan afterlife used to reconstruct 
aspects of Minoan religious conceptions.", "source_ids": 
["20260628153822814854_789fd2dd"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": 
"20260628153851095317_4493862a", "type": "Claim", "text": "On stylistic grounds,
Evans assigns the Ring of Nestor to the Late Bronze Age Aegean glyptic 
tradition, arguing that its engraving is closely related to Minoan and Mycenaean
art and exemplifies cultural connections between Crete and mainland Greece.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628153822814854_789fd2dd", 
"20260628153822989244_84d15031"], "citations": [8, 13]}, {"id": 
"20260628153851245033_02210c0d", "type": "Claim", "text": "A cluster of modern 
scholarly discussions (Evans 1925 and 1930, Pini 1998, Krzyszkowska 2005) treat 
the Ring of Nestor’s engraved scene as non-realistic and mythological rather 
than depicting an actual historical event.", "source_ids": 
["20260628153822877550_2fda6fc9"], "citations": [10]}, {"id": 
"20260628153851342494_adf41576", "type": "Claim", "text": "Pini’s 1998 
peer-reviewed article \"The 'Ring of Nestor'\" undertakes a technical 
examination of the ring and presents new observations that are explicitly 
claimed to support its authenticity as an ancient artefact, situating the piece 
within the corpus of Minoan–Mycenaean gold signet rings and refining its 
chronological and stylistic placement as Late Bronze Age Aegean.", "source_ids":
["20260628153822843474_07c7f954"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628153851497303_ecb6fe52", "type": "Claim", "text": "Modern scholarship 
includes a dedicated study, described on Academia.edu, that analyzes the 
iconography of the Ring of Nestor—its figures, chariot and landscape elements—in
comparison with other Aegean glyptic scenes, and places this analysis within 
broader debates about Aegean forgeries and the ring’s authenticity, engaging 
with Evans (1925) and Pini (1998).", "source_ids": 
["20260628153737636391_c117a38b"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628153851645662_6869736b", "type": "Claim", "text": "The Ring of Nestor 
has been repeatedly linked in modern literature to the problem of Aegean 
forgeries and doubts about its genuineness, with some studies treating it among 
suspected forgeries while others, notably Evans and Pini, argue in favor of its 
authenticity based on stylistic and technical considerations.", "source_ids": 
["20260628153737636391_c117a38b", "20260628153822814854_789fd2dd", 
"20260628153822843474_07c7f954"], "citations": [4, 8, 9]}, {"id": 
"20260628153851814151_bb8233e9", "type": "Claim", "text": "Evans’s broader 
discussions of the Minoan world use signet rings and bead-seals from Thisbê and 
other sites (including the Ring of Nestor, the so-called Thisbê jewel, and 
parallels from Knossos and the Fourth Shaft Grave at Mycenae) to argue for 
stylistic and cultural links between Minoan Crete, mainland Greece and regions 
such as Egypt.", "source_ids": ["20260628153822989244_84d15031"], "citations": 
[13]}], "offset": 0, "limit": 20, "total": 17, "next_offset": null}
