{"query": {"id": "20260628153519740118_689d9dfb", "handle": "q2", "text": "“Ring
of Nestor” archaeological object discovery description", "importance": "core"}, 
"items": [{"id": "20260628153739405685_fdd0d7b0", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"Evans introduces “a truly royal treasure of gold rings and of bead-seals in the
same metal” found in “a Mycenaean rock-cut chamber tomb near the site of Thisbê 
in Boeotia, by the village of Dombrena.”", "source_ids": 
["20260628153737564766_3888b0b0"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628153739469199_36b6af55", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans notes that 
the treasure “was discovered in 1915” when “war conditions diverted the course 
of discovery from official channels,” and that it was “made by a peasant in a 
rock-cut chamber tomb,” with some details withheld for reasons of confidence.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628153737564766_3888b0b0"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628153739526687_2a14f269", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans singles out 
“one especially large gold signet ring” which he dubs “the Ring of Nestor,” 
describing its “beaded gold hoop” and “large oval bezel” engraved with “three 
successive scenes” in Minoan style.", "source_ids": 
["20260628153737564766_3888b0b0"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628153739583265_67212593", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans interprets 
the bezel’s scene as “a Minoan-type ritual or funerary scene in an ‘after-world’
setting,” involving deities, altars, and symbols suggestive of “the Minoan cult 
of the dead.”", "source_ids": ["20260628153737564766_3888b0b0"], "citations": 
[1]}, {"id": "20260628153739639260_eee5e212", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans
mentions associated finds such as “bronze spear-heads and a short sword of a 
very late Mycenaean type,” which he uses to place the treasure chronologically 
in the Late Helladic period.", "source_ids": ["20260628153737564766_3888b0b0"], 
"citations": [1]}, {"id": "20260628153739699599_ea1e9a20", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The SciSpace record lists the article title as “The ring of Nestor: a 
glimpse into the Minoan after-world and a sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia,” providing journal and 
citation information.", "source_ids": ["20260628153737588459_9f56b438"], 
"citations": [2]}, {"id": "20260628153739758133_afe676f3", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Hudler describes the Ring of Nestor as “polarizing study object and 
dubitanda,” placing it among Aegean Bronze Age finds whose authenticity is 
doubted.", "source_ids": ["20260628153737611860_9bd78111"], "citations": [3]}, 
{"id": "20260628153739821432_0d2db54b", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Hudler 
notes that the ring is “allegedly discovered in a tholos tomb in Kakovatos near 
Pylos” in some narratives, in contrast to the more widely known association with
Thisbê in Boeotia.", "source_ids": ["20260628153737611860_9bd78111"], 
"citations": [3]}, {"id": "20260628153739882191_0e866b34", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Hudler explains that the ring’s provenance and authenticity have been 
questioned, especially in light of known forgeries and workshop practices in the
early 20th-century Aegean antiquities market, and that some scholars class it as
dubitanda.", "source_ids": ["20260628153737611860_9bd78111"], "citations": [3]},
{"id": "20260628153739943646_da4a5eaa", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
Academia.edu paper explicitly links the Ring of Nestor to the broader “Aegean 
forgeries” problem and questions whether its intricate iconography and execution
are consistent with securely provenanced Bronze Age rings.", "source_ids": 
["20260628153737636391_c117a38b"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628153740008383_b30794a8", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Cambridge 
epilogue refers to the “Discovery of the Royal Signet-ring; Beaded gold hoop 
resembling that of ‘Ring of Nestor’; Parallel arrangement of designs—three 
